or out the common factor of 2:\n\\[\n2 \\left( \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right).\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the known values:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\) and \\(\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\). Using angle sum identities:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{3} - \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{12} \\right) = \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right).\n\\]\n\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[\n2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2} + 2\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = 2 \\left( \\frac{2\\sqrt{6} + 2\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
